TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "action": "final",
  "output": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "Opponent next-possession points per possession (PPP) conditional on preceding offensive play type",
      "AVOID labels derived from defensive outcomes of play types",
      "Own-sequence lag effects: team's own next-possession PPP after running a specific play type",
      "Play-type classification reliability from play-by-play data",
      "Cross-season stability of play-type effects on subsequent possessions"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "Sample size and statistical power to detect between-play-type differences in opponent next-possession PPP",
      "Confounding by game state (score margin, period, time remaining, opponent strength, lineup quality)",
      "Multiple testing across many distinct play types",
      "Measurement reliability of derived play-type labels from NBA play-by-play",
      "Regression to the mean for play types with extreme observed next-possession PPP",
      "Look-ahead bias when labeling plays retroactively based on future PPP outcomes",
      "Sparsity of rare play types leading to unstable PPP estimates",
      "Non-stationarity across seasons and offensive/defensive trends"
    ],
    "historical_evidence": [],
    "failed_approaches": [],
    "evidence_establishes": [],
    "evidence_does_not_establish": [
      "No prior studies retrieved for this specific question; the relationship between play type and opponent next-possession PPP, or own-sequence lag PPP, has not been previously assessed within this project's evidence base."
    ],
    "own_inferences": [
      "The data are sufficient in principle to compute opponent next-possession PPP and own next-possession PPP following each identified play type, using play-by-play and derived play-type possessions.",
      "Common play types (pick-and-roll ball handler, isolation, post-up, spot-up, transition) have enough possessions across four seasons to allow detection of moderate effect sizes, but rare play types (e.g., off-screen, hand-off) likely lack power.",
      "Large confounding is expected from game situation, opponent defensive quality, and lineup strength; without careful adjustment, apparent differences may be artifacts.",
      "Multiple comparisons across dozens of play types require stringent correction, which will further reduce effective power for rarer types.",
      "The own-sequence lag analysis adds a separate set of comparisons but is conceptually similar and faces the same methodological issues.",
      "The AVOID label itself is an outcome-derived category; if it is defined solely by high opponent next-possession PPP from the same data, it is descriptive rather than predictive, and care must be taken not to overinterpret in-sample patterns."
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Actual granularity and accuracy of play-type labels from the graph database is unknown; misclassification could dilute or bias estimates.",
      "The operational definition of 'AVOID label'—threshold PPP, minimum sample size, and decision rule—is not specified in the question.",
      "The extent to which next-possession outcomes are influenced by the preceding play type versus the many confounders cannot be known without analysis.",
      "The temporal stability of effects across the 2022–2026 period is uncertain; rule changes or style trends may alter relationships."
    ],
    "historical_effect": {
      "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
      "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so the decision rests solely on the assessment of data adequacy, testability, and methodological risks."
    },
    "testability": {
      "p_testable": 0.7,
      "blocking_constraints": [
        {
          "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
          "text": "Rare play types (e.g., handoff, miscellaneous) may have fewer than 1000 opponent next-possessions, making PPP estimates too noisy to support reliable AVOID labels."
        },
        {
          "code": "CONFOUNDING",
          "text": "Game context (score, period, opponent, lineup) strongly influences next-possession outcomes; without identifying assumptions, separating the causal effect of play type is challenging."
        },
        {
          "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
          "text": "Testing dozens of play types simultaneously inflates false positives; correction reduces power, especially for marginal effect sizes."
        }
      ]
    },
    "prior_work": {
      "already_answered": "unknown",
      "cited": [],
      "summary": "No earlier studies on this topic were available in the retrieval results. The question appears not to have been directly addressed in prior work by this project."
    },
    "risks": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Statistical power will be insufficient for infrequently used play types, leading to unreliable AVOID classifications based solely on observed PPP."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Opponent next-possession PPP is influenced by score differential, period, timeout usage, defensive matchups, and back-to-back schedules, all correlated with play-type selection."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Evaluating all play types for both opponent next-possession PPP and own-sequence PPP involves potentially >100 hypothesis tests, raising false discovery risk."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play-type labels derived from play-by-play may misclassify possessions (e.g., transition vs. secondary break) introducing measurement error that attenuates or biases effect estimates."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Play types with low usage (e.g., alley-oop, post-up for non-centers) yield very few next-possession events within a season, making season-specific AVOID labels impossible."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "If AVOID labels are defined using PPP outcomes from the very possessions used to measure the effect, the label is circular; future predictive validity would require out-of-sample testing."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "Play types identified as extreme (best or worst) in one sample will tend to regress toward the mean in a subsequent sample, potentially invalidating AVOID labels."
      },
      {
        "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
        "text": "Relationships may change across the four seasons due to coaching, rule changes, or offensive trends, complicating aggregation and interpretation."
      }
    ],
    "predicted_outcome": {
      "supported": 0.25,
      "rejected": 0.15,
      "untestable": 0.15,
      "artifact": 0.10,
      "uncertain": 0.35
    },
    "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
    "design": {
      "estimand": "For each distinct play type, the mean opponent points per possession on the opponent's immediately following possession, adjusted for game-state covariates; analogously, the mean team's own PPP on its next possession after running that play type.",
      "unit": "One possession: the next possession (by opponent, or by own team after regaining the ball) following a completed offensive possession with an identified play type. Exclude possessions following end-of-quarter/game and technical free throws.",
      "sample": "All regular-season and playoff games from NBA seasons 2022-23 through 2025-26 available as of 2026-06-19. Only play types with at least 500 occurrences in the pooled data are considered for AVOID labels; rarer types are reported descriptively.",
      "power": "For common play types (>10,000 occurrences), simulation-based power >80% to detect an opponent next-possession PPP difference of 0.03 (about 0.3 points per 10 possessions) at α=0.05 before multiplicity adjustment. For moderate types (2000–5000 occurrences), power to detect 0.05 PPP difference. Formal power simulation using historical PPP variance will be preregistered before analysis.",
      "controls": "Mixed-effects model with fixed effects for play type, and covariates: score margin (linear and quadratic), period (categorical), time remaining in quarter (restricted cubic spline), opponent defensive rating (season-long), possession start type (rebound, turnover, made basket, dead ball), home/away, rest days, and lineup strength (estimated via ridge regression on current players on court). Random intercepts for team, opponent, and season. Own-sequence models additionally control for whether the team retained possession on an offensive rebound.",
      "falsification": "1. Placebo test: randomly permute the play-type labels among possessions and re-estimate; the distribution of effect sizes should be centered at zero. 2. Temporal stability: split the data into early/late seasons and test whether AVOID-flagged play types replicate. 3. Leave-one-season-out cross-validation of AVOID labels.",
      "multiplicity": "Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate control at 10% across all play types tested for opponent next-possession PPP and own-sequence PPP separately. Bayesian hierarchical models will be used for shrinkage and posterior-based inference as a sensitivity analysis.",
      "decision_rule": "A play type qualifies for an AVOID label if (a) the adjusted opponent next-possession PPP is higher than the league-wide mean PPP with a one-sided false-discovery-rate-corrected p < 0.05 AND the estimated excess is at least 0.03 PPP; (b) the result replicates in a holdout season. Own-sequence lag effects are evaluated analogously: a play type is flagged as SELF-HURT if own next-possession PPP is significantly lower than average by at least 0.03 PPP. Joint AVOID+SELF-HURT types are highlighted for coaching consideration.",
      "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 per-game play-by-play data and derived play-type possession records as stored in the project's graph database, accessed on 2026-06-19. Exact data snapshots to be hashed and archived."
    }
  }
}

